    "D",
    "E",
    "F"
  ],
  "correct": "A",
  "hints": [
    {
      "title": "Expand the square before integrating",
      "body": "<p>Use <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>v</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>u</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msup><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>4</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. The middle product is constant because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</p>",
      "recap": "Expanding a squared expression can turn an integral into a sum of powers.",
      "pitfall": "Squaring a difference includes a negative cross term; it is not just the sum of the two squares.",
      "pause": "Write the integrand as three powers of x.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l20",
          "reminder": "Simplify to separate powers before using the integration power rule."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Increase each exponent, then divide",
      "body": "<p>The expansion is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>16</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. Use <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0222B;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>r</mi></msup><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. For the last term, the new exponent is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>.</p>",
      "recap": "The integration power rule works for negative exponents other than minus one.",
      "pitfall": "Dividing by the new exponent means the antiderivative of the last term has a negative coefficient.",
      "pause": "Find an antiderivative and differentiate it to check the signs.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l20",
          "reminder": "The integration power rule works for negative exponents other than minus one."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Subtract the entire lower-limit value",
      "body": "<p>Use your own antiderivative to evaluate the upper-limit value and the lower-limit value separately. Keep the entire lower-limit value in brackets while subtracting.</p>",
      "recap": "A definite integral is the upper-limit value minus the lower-limit value.",
      "pitfall": "The lower limit is 1, so its contribution is not zero. Subtract all three of its terms.",
      "pause": "Evaluate the two values exactly, then collect the fractions.",
      "recall": [
        {
          "lessonId": "p1-b1-l20",
          "reminder": "A definite integral is the upper-limit value minus the lower-limit value."
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
  "solution": "<p><strong>1. Expand into powers.</strong> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>4</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>16</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>2. Integrate each power.</strong> An antiderivative is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>5</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>8</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>16</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>3. Evaluate upper minus lower.</strong> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>32</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>16</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>5</mn></mfrac><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>16</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mrow></math>. This simplifies to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>31</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>5</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>14</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>93</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>120</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>70</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>15</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>43</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>15</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>Answer A.</strong> The cross term and the subtraction of the lower limit are both essential.</p>",
